#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "c02d2000-3fef-59e4-9127-84500ef83eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-418" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n\\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "c7a90d29-e8b9-5a06-94f7-776eb254cc52" :question/math-academy-id "e-419" :question/problem "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54 },$ what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg( \\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x }?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{0}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 54+x\\,{|}_{6}^{0} \\\\  & =18+(0-6) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "a85f51c5-601b-52ec-82dd-0b611842d3d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-420" :question/problem "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+18={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}(2f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(12)+x\\,{|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =24+(10-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078227 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "3d925b89-dc4d-59e0-bf00-3a3ca90b5169" :question/math-academy-id "e-421" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$ \nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}.$  What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}2\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be7db794-612b-5880-8442-05b9cbe68aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287176} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec2f26d-1d9d-5499-a6b7-f7ce0454858c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287177} {:answer/id #uuid "4a675bb6-86b1-57a2-b204-c11b82ed4ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287178} {:answer/id #uuid "140cdff0-9393-536c-a099-b1d21dd3498b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287179} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b97efb-9a57-5f06-8a15-4af798f97a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3b97efb-9a57-5f06-8a15-4af798f97a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287180} :answer-field/id #uuid "0856f487-a3c2-5792-890b-c3a148c879db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11d4d7cf-8fc6-50cc-adb4-6da8f5e57392" :question/math-academy-id "q-114006" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8d13c74-fcd7-50bf-8e98-4bdee8efc3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298327} {:answer/id #uuid "68a0d81b-531e-5a36-916b-c9c08d33da89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298328} {:answer/id #uuid "69d810ec-9162-5d1a-a5ae-1e6cabea483e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298329} {:answer/id #uuid "47d03ecc-fb00-5ec9-bb1a-043a62257a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298330} {:answer/id #uuid "07b15ea4-bf03-5902-8c46-d403337be569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d13c74-fcd7-50bf-8e98-4bdee8efc3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298327} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f30d74-b006-54e8-b153-75393e79b65e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30144ba3-8434-5a5c-af65-968cf2badda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114016" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot x\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-6 \\\\  & =8(3-1)-6 \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2-6 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69ea1e3d-dc06-55ac-96cb-198a85cb5968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298334} {:answer/id #uuid "40cebf71-b610-58c4-bb42-4fb90ef0f8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298335} {:answer/id #uuid "9e86906b-6482-5407-b3fb-54277787c5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298336} {:answer/id #uuid "09d10639-77fe-5cb0-9ab7-f266159f785e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298337} {:answer/id #uuid "f61c1445-0106-59a7-b683-c9035bab95a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f61c1445-0106-59a7-b683-c9035bab95a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298338} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bdfebfc-1c3e-5a02-9e91-b37099a84576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61ddbb4e-de49-5742-b133-eb07be0fd7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-114138" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1,$ then what is ${\\int }_{-1}^{3}(2f(x)-3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+1 & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 3 & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(2f(x)-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-3(3-(-1)) \\\\  & =6-3(4) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dad3368-3b1f-5f30-adb6-5b54b1058bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287225} {:answer/id #uuid "d454aabc-56ca-5c64-970a-a6847e824c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287226} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} {:answer/id #uuid "b5880e00-93d8-50ca-b06b-d51e42517cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287228} {:answer/id #uuid "2b23a4e3-3cfe-5e99-bd3a-33291a12abb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c48bd3e-22c1-5da5-929b-92235c89e5ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700eca56-7546-5c46-a5c9-5b46500fdf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-114143" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ 3{\\int }_{8}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ -3{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe5c6322-a33e-5a7f-8039-937d469dbdf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298341} {:answer/id #uuid "12aa52ba-29e3-50a3-9543-de6a3834c635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298342} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9560cb-8f32-5005-957c-e2a5397f366a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298343} {:answer/id #uuid "8237aab3-9a6d-55c8-be4f-a24490c93905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298344} {:answer/id #uuid "04326036-7645-5b05-84da-8e78cc586097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9560cb-8f32-5005-957c-e2a5397f366a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298343} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eee6eee-0177-5cb2-b1df-c5fa9e98ace8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79362d8f-ee8e-5edf-a603-e0c270628ab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-114153" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+(-6) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d4d4ced-0c0a-5e16-8013-01b1e210034a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d4d4ced-0c0a-5e16-8013-01b1e210034a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298348} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5c793e-ad0d-53a8-94c6-b58ec368a1fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "677c30fe-59e7-5678-acb0-6b7479c8d2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345628" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c07373-2118-5f77-8e3c-f0b76f884aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298351} {:answer/id #uuid "9f178c27-18ef-5200-923d-2777a7c53952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f178c27-18ef-5200-923d-2777a7c53952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298352} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd4601b-0aee-56a8-9184-73bfa7cbcb67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ed72a9d-c344-5a6e-a8f7-e12e785b6c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-345629" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{4}{2}^{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{4}{2}^{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d39061c-5998-5f0a-97a0-67a9eacaec72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d39061c-5998-5f0a-97a0-67a9eacaec72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298355} :answer-field/id #uuid "1812e228-52dc-5fe2-81f3-feb08a610fa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a01d0d55-496a-59f0-876c-6cb1db4e698e" :question/math-academy-id "q-345634" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{0}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{0}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7fbd40-a307-5e2a-9ca7-da314561c49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7fbd40-a307-5e2a-9ca7-da314561c49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298358} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2e93b8-c705-5690-ac25-ecf35f01f174" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a789764d-6c23-5081-bf29-e1cefd94e0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-346059" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{0}\\,-\\,(-6) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{0-9}{2}\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{2}\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7bde330-93f8-5288-895c-b37828061946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298361} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9ba678-6070-5c6a-bfb8-5719cbb17d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc9ba678-6070-5c6a-bfb8-5719cbb17d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298362} :answer-field/id #uuid "4db99a97-3fbd-5b86-9827-4fa550b42518" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06db151f-c6d6-553d-91fa-0c48006d8e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-346061" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{-6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-6}^{6}(3x-9f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{6}(3x-9f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{-6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}(3x-9f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-6}^{6}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,9{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-6}^{6}\\,-\\,9(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{6}^{2}-(-6)^{2}}{2}\\,+\\,9 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0\\,+\\,9 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3a01c27-b258-5f2f-8181-05b3000f48a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365} {:answer/id #uuid "522d036b-4e1a-5bf6-94e7-e1a343f49cc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a01c27-b258-5f2f-8181-05b3000f48a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c7b002-864b-5b0e-b82d-79cd606e879a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "443dc647-fc7d-5181-add0-610b67501f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346181" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{113}{9},$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{113}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,8{\\int }_{-3}^{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{113}{9})\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{6} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,108 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1866726-5e6e-5d37-ae66-77a5dd027662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1866726-5e6e-5d37-ae66-77a5dd027662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298369} :answer-field/id #uuid "06568dec-b3e7-55d9-9f5b-4bc42d6c1c9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a02e085b-165a-538f-ae55-e825e6b5f89f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346186" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3$ and ${\\int }_{7}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-10,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{2}^{11}(2-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{11}(2-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{7}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n3+(-10)\\,=\\,{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{11}(2-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{2}^{11}1\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,6{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot x{|}_{2}^{11}\\,-\\,6(-7) \\\\  & =2(11-2)+42 \\\\  & =18+42 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01080b51-c0d9-5a7d-9e36-5bc67d4b4cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01080b51-c0d9-5a7d-9e36-5bc67d4b4cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298372} :answer-field/id #uuid "55dc33ab-63c5-507f-9f01-dd46c490a9e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abacc09f-e24f-5083-9902-ea5b1af36c84" :question/math-academy-id "q-346187" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{6}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=11,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n(-1)+11={\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=10.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{4}^{10}1\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot x{|}_{4}^{10}-3(10) \\\\  & =6(10-4)-30 \\\\  & =36-30 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad292ea9-dd5f-5514-b897-9d26801459df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298375} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4e0dc-2414-5dbd-a02c-4ed071f332f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298376} {:answer/id #uuid "ee03515b-63c9-5a57-a099-0a91174bb038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298377} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c8eb6d-129d-5988-aaeb-86043df1b923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298378} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3b158d-7679-5c83-ba2f-9c82989ff367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3b158d-7679-5c83-ba2f-9c82989ff367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298379} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d599d0-48dd-5bd4-bca8-58ce8dd874e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e39e611c-2e6a-5118-851f-9558e33dfe9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4726" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c927b5b8-66d3-52db-8bf0-b3a531fcdb88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298382} {:answer/id #uuid "e0df4282-89c7-5050-b0b3-ff7615b118f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298383} {:answer/id #uuid "33463087-38f5-577d-89f7-5f2eced21970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298384} {:answer/id #uuid "e37a82c1-e8f4-56a4-87a1-0f2f6a47f9b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298385} {:answer/id #uuid "81a5eb46-1077-557a-8954-1b42e0011210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81a5eb46-1077-557a-8954-1b42e0011210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298386} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac64c44-89a2-555d-a305-fdb2250b1233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae339ff6-1422-5622-973f-bdc1e90b872b" :question/math-academy-id "q-4789" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=9$ and ${\\int }_{9}^{6}4\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=20.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{2}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{9}^{6}4\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =20 \\\\ 4{\\int }_{9}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =20 \\\\ -4{\\int }_{6}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =20 \\\\ {\\int }_{6}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9+(-5) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e8904f-7cbe-5fcf-8a34-e9861f46e3f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298389} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a1b409-1a13-52dc-93b6-19a7061f9933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298390} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ee562e-1a00-517d-81a4-7041ea4d844f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298391} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d95299-ac10-5eb9-b5f6-19067958467c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} {:answer/id #uuid "5a48057a-c503-527e-b209-24eb83285ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a48057a-c503-527e-b209-24eb83285ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298393} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ae0418-fa3d-59f6-8cd8-b0a00150d751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f7e1e9b-4799-51a0-a138-eeebd44e608b" :question/math-academy-id "q-4795" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-2)+8 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a6bc206-723a-5b18-96fe-2dacab5be57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076912} {:answer/id #uuid "b840df94-5b44-50f8-b839-9c5395188555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076913} {:answer/id #uuid "da4036ff-85dc-546e-8765-bd5f0de7b0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076914} {:answer/id #uuid "418d607c-7f32-5f79-b898-4b01862e0864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076915} {:answer/id #uuid "7dacf899-f625-5aed-958e-c5eed026011b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da4036ff-85dc-546e-8765-bd5f0de7b0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076914} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39566f9-0d68-5afa-b90d-e5a65e2c1a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2090ed89-1b9f-5b68-b522-52c6e26b20f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-49240" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{3}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{3}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1a5a7b6-531a-5ff4-8405-b8a1fe96fa21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076924} {:answer/id #uuid "c64d7734-2de6-57de-8d2a-2731308435b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076925} {:answer/id #uuid "e52da3f9-a20a-5a1c-a2e4-f824a55b8e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076926} {:answer/id #uuid "1240027a-50b3-5851-9247-48769705f8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076927} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed5199d-4758-5197-aa5d-8e18f23d2e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1240027a-50b3-5851-9247-48769705f8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076927} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb03473-bb37-5148-a670-10ca89f111ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51cf8206-19a8-5955-b06e-e262cf75d9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49245" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{9}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6$, calculate\n${\\int }_{4}^{9}(4f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{9}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}(4f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{4}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(-6)+2\\cdot x\\,{|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =-24+2(9-4) \\\\  & =-24+10 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4a42881-aada-5097-ae2b-d87549c783dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298396} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf8abf3-876a-5c2f-9409-3ae2364263a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298397} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d3b677-ee17-5c75-9ff7-751cb49a4e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298398} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb4a0d3-182a-53a9-b953-e917f251a481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298399} {:answer/id #uuid "3794a708-d038-5027-a85b-7218f4806df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb4a0d3-182a-53a9-b953-e917f251a481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298399} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcf66a86-20a0-5a33-8c18-bb5e8a846bb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "214f9d44-39be-5f83-8d3d-a2d75b81055a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49249" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{7}(7+3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5)+8={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 3={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(7+3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{7}+3(3) \\\\  & =7(7-0)+9 \\\\  & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34252ab6-fa19-5895-a0e0-0dc3a26dfb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c340bc5-4510-59f3-945b-72aaa54dacb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298404} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6b8493-9750-5e2b-b79e-f0a945888ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298405} {:answer/id #uuid "2b81ae64-1626-5219-922b-3aab9508aef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298406} {:answer/id #uuid "f2db9976-3a9a-5eaf-921f-70211aaea110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c340bc5-4510-59f3-945b-72aaa54dacb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298404} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a38726-a848-5322-86a1-54290e3fc398" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b061a43c-1108-5502-a0d5-189a5c9a0b07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73350" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+\\frac{1}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{16}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{16}{3})+3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =16+3(6-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
